# Scripts

A **script** is a program in a file that runs as a command: its first line
names `hql` as its interpreter, the shell runs it by its path, and the words
after the path are its arguments. The reading club keeps its shelf in a
program, and its librarian wants one command for the question asked before
every meeting: which books are still to read?

```text
$ ./todo.hql
The Hobbit
Leaves of Grass
$ ./todo.hql reading
Dune
```

A script is checked before it runs, as every program is. Its arguments are
text, and it converts them as it converts any text. Its result is presented
as `hql run` presents it, and the result also gives the **exit status** the
shell reads.

## A first script

A script opens with an **interpreter line**, `#!` and the command that runs
it. `hql` skips that line, and the program starts on the next:

```text
$ cat todo.hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
import io.env

type Book { title: String, status: String }

shelf = [
    Book(title = "Frankenstein", status = "done"),
    Book(title = "Dune", status = "reading"),
    Book(title = "The Hobbit", status = "todo"),
    Book(title = "Leaves of Grass", status = "todo"),
]
wanted = env.argv()[0] | unwrap_or("todo")
shelf | filter(b => b.status == wanted) | map(b => b.title) | sink(stdout, Text)
$ chmod +x todo.hql
$ ./todo.hql
The Hobbit
Leaves of Grass
```

`./todo.hql` makes the kernel run `/usr/bin/env hql ./todo.hql`, and
`hql ./todo.hql` runs the file:

```d2
direction: down

shell: "./todo.hql reading" {style.fill: "#f6efe6"}
kernel: "/usr/bin/env hql ./todo.hql reading" {style.fill: "#f6efe6"}
run: "hql run ./todo.hql -- reading" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
argv: "env.argv() == [\"reading\"]" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
status: "exit status from the result" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}

shell -> kernel: "#! line"
kernel -> run: "a word with /"
run -> argv: "the words after the file"
run -> status
```

The interpreter line is skipped only at the very start of a file. Anywhere
else `#!` is a syntax error, since `#` is not a token in HQL.

A diagnostic still counts the interpreter line as line 1, so a mistake on
the next line is reported on line 2, as an editor shows it.

## Arguments

`env.argv()` is the script's **positional arguments**: the words after the
program, in order, as a `Seq<String>`. The script's own path is not among
them. The program below runs as `./todo.hql reading`:

<!-- run: novault -- reading -->
```hql
import io.env

env.argv()
```

```text
["reading"]
```

`env.argv()[0]` is the first word, an `Option<String>`, so a script with no
arguments supplies its default with `unwrap_or`. This is the program of
`todo.hql`, run with `reading`:

<!-- run: --to text -- reading -->
```hql
import io.env

type Book { title: String, status: String }

shelf = [
    Book(title = "Frankenstein", status = "done"),
    Book(title = "Dune", status = "reading"),
    Book(title = "The Hobbit", status = "todo"),
    Book(title = "Leaves of Grass", status = "todo"),
]
wanted = env.argv()[0] | unwrap_or("todo")
shelf | filter(b => b.status == wanted) | map(b => b.title)
```

```text
Dune
```

Every word after the program belongs to the program, options included. In
`./todo.hql --to json`, `--to json` is two words of the script's, not an
option of `hql`. An option for `hql` comes before the program. `--bare`
runs the script without looking for a project:

```text
$ hql --bare todo.hql reading
Dune
```

Named arguments, `--arg name=value`, are options of `hql` too, so they come
before the program as well. They are read with `env.arg(name)`
([[environment|environment and arguments]]). Positional arguments are
always granted, whatever the project's `env` grant says: the person who ran
the script wrote them.

## Ways to run a program

A script is one of several ways to hand `hql` a program. Each one has a
named command, and the short forms are the ones a shell user knows from
Python and Bash:

| Command line | Runs | Same as |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `./todo.hql reading` | the file, through its `#!` line | `hql ./todo.hql reading` |
| `hql todo.hql reading` | the file: the word ends in `.hql` | `hql run todo.hql reading` |
| `hql -c 'PROGRAM' a b` | the program text | `hql eval 'PROGRAM' a b` |
| `hql 'PROGRAM'` | the program text | `hql eval 'PROGRAM'` |
| `echo 'PROGRAM' \| hql` | standard input, when it is not a terminal | `hql run -` |
| `hql` | the REPL, on a terminal | `hql repl` |

A word is a file only when it is written as one: it ends in `.hql`, or it
contains a `/` and names an existing file. `hql todo` evaluates `todo` as
an expression even when a file named `todo` is in the directory. A file
placed in a directory cannot turn a typed expression into a program run. A
`#!` invocation always passes a path with a `/`, so every script runs.

A command name wins over a file of that name: `hql run` is the command, and
`hql ./run` is the file.

## The exit status

A shell reads a command's **exit status** to decide what happens next:
`./check.hql && ./publish.hql` publishes only when the check passed. HQL
has no operation that sets the status. The status follows from the
program's result:

| The program | Exit status |
| --- | --- |
| fails to parse, check or evaluate | `1` |
| has a result that is `err(e)` | `1` |
| has any other result | `0` |
| is invoked wrongly, a usage error | `2` |

A script that can fail says so in its result type, a `Result`. This
version of `todo.hql` fails when no book has the status asked for. It runs
as `./todo.hql lent`:

<!-- run: novault -- lent -->
```hql
import io.env

type Book { title: String, status: String }

shelf = [
    Book(title = "Dune", status = "reading"),
    Book(title = "The Hobbit", status = "todo"),
]
wanted = env.argv()[0] | unwrap_or("todo")
left = shelf | filter(b => b.status == wanted) | map(b => b.title)
ok(left) given left != [] otherwise err("no book is " + wanted)
```

```text
err("no book is lent")
```

The result is still printed, and the shell sees the failure:

```text
$ ./todo.hql lent; echo "exit $?"
err("no book is lent")
exit 1
$ ./todo.hql todo; echo "exit $?"
ok(["The Hobbit"])
exit 0
```

Only the result itself is tested. An `err` inside a list or a record is
data, so `[ok(1), err("x")]` exits `0`.

## Relative paths

A relative path in HQL resolves against the folder of the source that
names it, never against the directory `hql` was started in. For a script,
that is the script's own folder:

<!-- run: skip -->
```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.fs import *

entries(folder("."))   // the script's folder, wherever it is run from
```

This is where HQL differs from the languages a script is usually written
in. Python, Node and Bash read `open("notes.txt")` and `ls .` against the
**working directory**, the directory the user stands in. HQL reads the
same path against the script's folder, as those languages resolve the
modules a file imports:

| | A module a file imports | A file a program reads |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Python | the script's folder | the working directory |
| Node | the file's folder | the working directory |
| Bash | the working directory | the working directory |
| HQL | the unit's folder | the unit's folder |

The same rule serves a document. A cell in a card resolves its paths
against the card's folder, wherever `hql render` was started, so a card
renders the same from the repository root, from its own folder, from CI
and from an editor. This is what notebook tools do too: Jupyter, knitr and
Quarto run a document's code in the document's folder. A script and a
cell follow one rule, and a program's answer depends on the program and
the files beside it, not on where it was run.

A script does want the working directory in one place: a path the user
types on the command line is named from where the user stands. The script
reads that directory with `fs.working_dir()` and joins the argument to it:

<!-- run: skip -->
```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.fs import *
import io.env

// Each folder named on the command line, or the directory the script was
// run from.
here = working_dir()
folders = env.argv() | map(p => folder(here.join(p))) given env.argv() != []
          otherwise [folder(here)]

folders | flat_map(f => entries(f)) | map(e => e.name) | sink(stdout, Text)
```

`join` keeps an absolute path as it is, so the same line handles both
kinds of argument:

<!-- run: novault -->
```hql
import io.fs

[Path("/home/ada").join("wiki"), Path("/home/ada").join("/tmp")]
```

```text
[Path(text = "/home/ada/wiki"), Path(text = "/tmp")]
```

This is `lsx.hql` from the
[script examples](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/tree/main/examples/scripts),
run from the reading club's folder. `books` and `authors` are named from
there, not from the script's folder:

```text
$ cd examples/reading-club
$ ../scripts/lsx.hql books authors
a-room-of-ones-own.hmd
dune.hmd
frankenstein.hmd
leaves-of-grass.hmd
mrs-dalloway.hmd
the-common-reader.hmd
the-hobbit.hmd
frank-herbert.hmd
jrr-tolkien.hmd
mary-shelley.hmd
virginia-woolf.hmd
walt-whitman.hmd
```

[[endpoints|Files and standard endpoints]] states where every kind of unit
resolves a relative path, and the [[env|reference]] states `argv` with the
rest of `io.env`.
